WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.829
In part one

2
00:00:03.829 --> 00:00:07.081
every split of the bikes lay on one line

3
00:00:07.158 --> 00:00:09.714
and a Markov matrix with positive

4
00:00:09.791 --> 00:00:12.888
entries shrank that line, day after day

5
00:00:12.888 --> 00:00:16.140
down to a single point: the steady state.

6
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.663
But real chains are full of zeros.

7
00:00:19.663 --> 00:00:23.210
Most pages link to only a few others.

8
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.151
With zeros allowed

9
00:00:25.151 --> 00:00:28.417
does the line still shrink to a point?

10
00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:29.840
Pause and think.

11
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.162
First zero pattern: two stations

12
00:00:36.245 --> 00:00:38.903
and every night every bike moves

13
00:00:38.986 --> 00:00:40.731
to the other station.

14
00:00:40.731 --> 00:00:43.306
That is the swap matrix: zero

15
00:00:43.306 --> 00:00:45.798
one in the first column, one

16
00:00:45.798 --> 00:00:47.460
zero in the second.

17
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.464
Start with all bikes at the market.

18
00:00:50.464 --> 00:00:53.800
After one night, they are all at the beach.

19
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.454
After two, all back at the market

20
00:00:56.454 --> 00:00:57.970
and so on, forever.

21
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.106
On the line of splits

22
00:01:00.106 --> 00:01:02.946
the swap does not shrink anything

23
00:01:02.946 --> 00:01:05.463
it flips the line end for end.

24
00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:07.005
The direction one

25
00:01:07.005 --> 00:01:09.845
minus one is sent to its negative

26
00:01:09.845 --> 00:01:12.930
an eigenvalue minus one, of size one

27
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.040
so that part never fades.

28
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.271
Half and half is still a steady state

29
00:01:18.271 --> 00:01:20.774
right in the middle of the line.

30
00:01:20.774 --> 00:01:24.110
But a start anywhere else never reaches it.

31
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.546
Second zero pattern

32
00:01:26.546 --> 00:01:28.958
a chain that falls apart.

33
00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:32.019
The simplest case: two stations

34
00:01:32.019 --> 00:01:35.452
and no bike ever leaves its station.

35
00:01:35.452 --> 00:01:38.050
The matrix is the identity.

36
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.229
Now nothing on the line moves at all.

37
00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:43.600
All at the market stays there.

38
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.429
Mostly at the beach stays there too.

39
00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:48.800
Every point is a steady state

40
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.400
the eigenvalue one appears twice.

41
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.228
With more stations

42
00:01:53.306 --> 00:01:56.574
the same happens whenever the chain splits

43
00:01:56.652 --> 00:01:59.609
into groups that never exchange bikes

44
00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:01.710
the start decides the end.

45
00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.730
The repair is random jumps.

46
00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.538
With probability d

47
00:02:06.538 --> 00:02:08.978
say zero point eight five

48
00:02:08.978 --> 00:02:11.509
follow the chain as before.

49
00:02:11.509 --> 00:02:12.503
Otherwise

50
00:02:12.503 --> 00:02:16.570
jump to a place chosen completely at random.

51
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.477
That mixes the matrix with one that

52
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.887
sends every split to the middle.

53
00:02:21.887 --> 00:02:24.444
Take the frozen chain: with jumps

54
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:26.417
one step pulls every point

55
00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.878
towards the middle

56
00:02:27.951 --> 00:02:30.142
and the line shrinks to eighty

57
00:02:30.215 --> 00:02:32.188
five percent of its length.

58
00:02:32.188 --> 00:02:34.160
Another step, and another.

59
00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:38.035
Ten steps leave only a fifth of the line.

60
00:02:38.035 --> 00:02:41.904
Every entry of the mixed matrix is positive

61
00:02:41.904 --> 00:02:45.257
so part one applies: one steady state

62
00:02:45.343 --> 00:02:47.579
and every other eigenvalue

63
00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:49.470
is at most d in size.

64
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.335
This is how a search engine can rank pages.

65
00:02:53.335 --> 00:02:56.085
Here is a made-up web of six pages.

66
00:02:56.085 --> 00:02:58.606
Page one links to two and three

67
00:02:58.606 --> 00:03:00.516
page two links to three

68
00:03:00.516 --> 00:03:03.113
and page three links back to one.

69
00:03:03.113 --> 00:03:05.787
Page four links to three and five

70
00:03:05.787 --> 00:03:07.849
page five to four and six

71
00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:09.530
and page six to five.

72
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.460
A random surfer on a page clicks

73
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.717
one of its links at random.

74
00:03:14.717 --> 00:03:18.103
That is a Markov matrix M, full of zeros.

75
00:03:18.183 --> 00:03:20.360
And once the surfer reaches

76
00:03:20.441 --> 00:03:21.972
pages one to three

77
00:03:21.972 --> 00:03:25.600
there is no link back to four, five and six.

78
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.676
So we add the jumps

79
00:03:27.676 --> 00:03:31.042
with d equal to zero point eight five.

80
00:03:31.042 --> 00:03:34.925
The length of each page's arrow is its rank.

81
00:03:34.925 --> 00:03:38.550
We start fair: every page gets one sixth.

82
00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:40.730
Count the links

83
00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.930
three pages link to page three.

84
00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:46.830
Page one gets a single link

85
00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.530
from page three.

86
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.867
Which page ranks first, and which second?

87
00:03:52.867 --> 00:03:54.520
Pause and guess.

88
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.147
A real web has billions of pages

89
00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:03.455
so nobody solves for the

90
00:04:03.547 --> 00:04:05.486
eigenvector directly.

91
00:04:05.486 --> 00:04:09.272
Instead: multiply by G, again and again.

92
00:04:09.272 --> 00:04:11.580
That is power iteration.

93
00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.642
One step: page three and page five grow

94
00:04:15.642 --> 00:04:17.406
the others shrink.

95
00:04:17.406 --> 00:04:20.376
Second step: page one jumps up

96
00:04:20.376 --> 00:04:24.088
because page three hands it everything.

97
00:04:24.088 --> 00:04:26.965
Third step: the arrows wobble

98
00:04:26.965 --> 00:04:28.450
less each time.

99
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.566
Keep going, and the arrows settle into

100
00:04:31.639 --> 00:04:34.551
the steady state of G: page three first

101
00:04:34.551 --> 00:04:36.880
with zero point two nine eight

102
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.991
and page one a close second

103
00:04:38.991 --> 00:04:41.320
with zero point two seven nine.

104
00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.105
Page one has just one incoming link

105
00:04:45.105 --> 00:04:47.955
but it comes from the top page

106
00:04:47.955 --> 00:04:51.071
and page three links nowhere else

107
00:04:51.071 --> 00:04:54.633
so it passes on almost all of its rank.

108
00:04:54.633 --> 00:04:57.660
Importance flows along the links.

109
00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.077
How fast does it settle?

110
00:05:00.163 --> 00:05:02.488
Each step shrinks the error

111
00:05:02.574 --> 00:05:04.640
by about three quarters

112
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.654
the size of the second eigenvalue.

113
00:05:07.654 --> 00:05:11.271
With jumps, that factor is never above d.

114
00:05:11.357 --> 00:05:13.682
After sixty steps the error

115
00:05:13.768 --> 00:05:15.490
is a few billionths.

116
00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.421
A habit for working with AI.

117
00:05:18.421 --> 00:05:21.023
Back at the two bike stations

118
00:05:21.107 --> 00:05:24.968
an assistant mixes up the market and the beach

119
00:05:25.052 --> 00:05:28.242
and reports the steady state as forty

120
00:05:28.242 --> 00:05:28.830
sixty.

121
00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.760
It adds up to one, so it sits on the line of

122
00:05:32.841 --> 00:05:34.631
splits and looks fine.

123
00:05:34.631 --> 00:05:38.129
But apply one more day: it moves to fifty

124
00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:38.698
fifty.

125
00:05:38.698 --> 00:05:41.220
A steady state would not move.

126
00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.257
Adding up to one is not enough.

127
00:05:44.257 --> 00:05:46.857
Say which convention you use

128
00:05:46.857 --> 00:05:49.370
and check that P v equals v.

129
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:50.827
To sum up.

130
00:05:50.827 --> 00:05:54.336
Zeros can stop the line from shrinking

131
00:05:54.336 --> 00:05:58.283
the swap flips it, a split chain freezes it.

132
00:05:58.371 --> 00:06:00.563
Random jumps shrink it by

133
00:06:00.651 --> 00:06:02.493
at least d each step

134
00:06:02.493 --> 00:06:06.177
and the point that stays is the PageRank

135
00:06:06.177 --> 00:06:09.510
found by multiplying again and again.

136
00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:10.847
Now you try

137
00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.809
find the steady state of the matrix

138
00:06:13.891 --> 00:06:16.277
with columns zero point nine

139
00:06:16.277 --> 00:06:19.238
zero point one and zero point four

140
00:06:19.238 --> 00:06:22.940
zero point six, and say how fast it settles.

141
00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.160
Next lecture: symmetric matrices

142
00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.440
whose eigenvectors meet at right angles.
